<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Temne people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Temne are a West African people of northern and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>, one of the country's two largest ethnic groups alongside the Mende. They speak a Mel language, and before colonial rule they were organised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> such as Koya on the Sierra Leone estuary, whose rulers granted the land on which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freetown">Freetown</a> was founded. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> spread among them from the 18th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/temne-people.jpg" alt="Temne people" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: gbaku · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TemneChildren.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Oral tradition places the Temne homeland in the Fouta Djallon highlands of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>. Bankole Taylor dates their migration as far back as the 11th or 12th century, after the fall of the Jallonkadu Empire, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> dominance in the 15th century drove them further south. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a> they forced the Limba northeast and the Bullom southwards and settled along the Rokel River down to the Sierra Leone River and Port Loko Creek. In the mid-16th century the Mane, a Mandé army from the east, conquered their lands, and the general Farma Tami ruled the Temne from Robaga, near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freetown">Freetown</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Portuguese records give the earliest mention of the Temne: Pacheco Pereira, writing between 1505 and 1508, noted their words for gold, water and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, and in 1594 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andre-alvares-de-almada">André Álvares de Almada</a> described village courts where advocates in masks pleaded before the chief. The Futa Jallon Jihad of the early 18th century brought slave raids, the sale of war captives into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic trade</a> and an influx of Susu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalunka">Yalunka</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naimbana">King Naimbana</a> of Koya, hostile to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> until his death in 1793, signed the treaty of 1788 giving the colonists the land his sub-chief Tom had ceded, and gave land and labour to the Black Nova Scotians who founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freetown">Freetown</a>. Koya fought Loko, Mende and Susu rulers between 1807 and 1888, and in 1889 the Temne lands fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. A hut tax due from 1 January 1898 set off the Hut Tax War of that year, in which the chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bai-bureh">Bai Bureh</a> took a leading part.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Temne language is related to the Baga language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>. The Temne are traditionally farmers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassava">cassava</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> and kola nut. Society is patrilineal and was organised in numerous independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> of villages, whose headmen usually inherited the post from the founder; in contemporary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a> chiefs are elected. A chief carries a sacred force, and the chiefdom spirit is represented by a mask whose wearer, the Sanko, appears with the chief throughout his life. Some clans had endogamous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a> of artisans and musicians, and enslaved people formed the lowest stratum.</p><p>Bankole Taylor estimates that 90% to 95% of the Temne are Muslims, while in the southeastern parts many have joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> with older beliefs in spirits and divination. Traditional religion centres on a Supreme Being, Kuru Masaba, approached through patrilineal ancestral spirits, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> take part in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-society">secret societies</a> such as the men's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poro">Poro</a> and Ragbenle and the women's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sande-society">Bondo</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Temne people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Temne</b> kingdom of Koya lay on the north and south shores of the Sierra Leone estuary.</blockquote><a href="/state/koya-temne-kingdom">Koya Temne kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>About 400 settlers sailed in 1787 and landed in May on land obtained from local Koya <b>Temne</b> rulers, where they founded Granville Town.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-freedom">Province of Freedom</a></li></ul></section>
